{
  "text": "Glial cells support neural signaling in several ways. Astrocytes help regulate the extracellular chemical environment, including potassium levels around active neurons. Oligodendrocytes form myelin around axons in the central nervous system and can myelinate segments of several axons. Schwann cells form myelin in the peripheral nervous system around one axon. Myelin increases electrical insulation between the nodes of Ranvier. Nodes of Ranvier are locations for action potential regeneration. The arrangement permits rapid saltatory conduction without requiring an action potential to be regenerated at every point beneath the myelin, whereas it does not require such regeneration at every point beneath the myelin. An axon is not always a myelinated axon; however, an unmyelinated axon can still conduct signal conduction. Damage to myelin can slow conduction of myelinated axons, reduce the speed of conduction of myelinated axons, and induce conduction failure in conduction failure of myelinated axons.",
  "reason": "The text was constructed by sequentially linking concepts via their defined relations: starting with glial cells supporting neural signaling, moving to astrocytes regulating the extracellular environment (including potassium), then distinguishing oligodendrocyte and schwann cell roles in forming myelin in different nervous systems. The structural benefits of myelin (insulation, nodes for regeneration) and the resulting conduction modes (saltatory vs. non-saltatory) were described using the 'arrangement' relations. Finally, the effects of damage to myelin on conduction speed and failure were appended, preserving all qualifiers like 'can', 'does not require', and 'is not always'."
